    {
      "id": "05-04",
      "title": "Routing versus forwarding",
      "chapter": "The routing puzzle",
      "duration": 21.166666666666668,
      "narration": "Routing decides which paths should populate a router’s forwarding information. Forwarding is the repeated local action of examining a packet and choosing an outgoing interface. A router does not usually run a complete shortest-path calculation for every arriving packet. It forwards using information already installed by its control plane.",
      "bullets": [
        "Routing builds the choices",
        "Forwarding uses those choices"
      ],
      "takeaway": "Compute routes in the control plane; forward packets using the result.",
