    {
      "id": "05-14",
      "title": "Distance vector: ask the neighbors",
      "chapter": "From algorithm to routers",
      "duration": 20.766666666666666,
      "narration": "Distance-vector routing takes a different approach. A router learns its neighbors’ advertised costs to destinations instead of first assembling the complete graph. It evaluates each neighbor’s reported destination cost plus the cost of reaching that neighbor. The smallest valid candidate determines a distance estimate and its next hop.",
      "bullets": [
        "Neighbor cost + advertised distance",
        "Choose the least-cost candidate"
